I didn't know that express doesn't listen for clientError events by default.
I just modified the basic example of each framework and put it back on kubernetes.

```
const express = require('express');
const http = require('http');
const app = express();
const port = 3000;
const server = http.createServer(app);
server.on('clientError', function (err) {
  console.error('on clientError handler');
  console.error(err);
});
app.get('/', (req, res) => res.send('Hello World!'));
server.listen(port, () => console.log(`Example app listening at http://localhost:${port}`));
```
```
const fastify = require('fastify')({logger: { level: debug }})
fastify.get('/', (request, reply) => {
  reply.send({ hello: 'world' })
})
fastify.listen(3000, (err, address) => {
  if (err) throw err
  fastify.log.info(`server listening on ${address}`)
})
```

Oh.. as you said, I saw the same error on express.
If these socket disconnection errors are common on the proxy, I think it's probably right to fastify to ignore those errors by default. What do you think? @mcollina

I had very similar problem with my application behind ingress-controller. I succesded found problem thank to express.js. Fastify on clientError returns always 400 response code, but we have two options in this case: 400 or 431 http code (https://nodejs.org/api/http.html#http_event_clienterror).

@xpender please try to add ``--max-http-header-size`` flag to node, probably in your case haproxy adds some headers to request and node.js isn't able to handle this request because headers size is too large.

@mcollina This case is easy to reproduce when header size is larger than 80KB
